`TreeTab.next` calls `self._nodes[self._focused].get_next_window()`. From the test, the add order plus the focus-sensitive insertion in `add_client`, followed by the listed `move_right`, `move_left`, `move_up`, and `move_down` operations, produces a tree where focused window `J` is a child of `B`, and `B` is the fifth top-level child in the section. In `TreeNode.get_next_window`, `J` has no children, so the loop climbs to parent `B`; there is no sibling after `J` there, so it climbs again to the section. At that level, `idx` is `4` for `B`, and the `for` loop checks the next position with `i = 5`; that sibling yields a first window, so the function returns through `if res:` with those loop-variable values.

